.TH AGENT-HOOK-PRE-BASH 1 "2026-09-29" "agentguard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-hook-pre-bash \- guard agent shell commands before execution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-hook-pre-bash
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-hook-pre-bash
reads an agent hook JSON payload from standard input and emits a hook JSON
response. It blocks or warns on risky shell commands such as broad destructive
removal, unsafe Git operations, unscoped recursive search, protected bare-Git
untracked scans, TTY-only commands, and other command patterns that should not
run unattended.
.PP
Manual-page reads with an explicit
.B cat
pager are allowed, for example
.BR "man -P cat tmux" ,
.BR "man --pager=cat tmux" ,
or
.BR "MANPAGER=cat man tmux" .
A literal command-prefix
.B PAGER=cat
is also recognized unless
.B MANPAGER
overrides it. Interactive pagers, nonempty
.BR MANOPT ,
and unsupported output modes remain blocked.
.PP
It also warns, without blocking, when a command runs a foreground
.B sleep
totalling 60 seconds or more (GNU duration operands such as
.BR 300 ,
.BR 5m ,
or
.B 1.5m
are summed), and points the agent at
.BR wait-until (1)
or the runtime's background execution. A sleep backgrounded with
.B &
(alone or in a backgrounded list or pipeline), or a tool call the host runs
in the background (Claude Code
.BR run_in_background ,
Gemini CLI
.BR is_background ),
stays silent. The payload of
.B wait-until \-\-cmd
is guarded like a
.B bash \-c
payload, since that is how it runs.
.PP
It warns when
.B git pr-watch \-\-wait
is launched detached (with
.BR & ,
.BR nohup ,
.BR setsid ,
or
.BR disown ),
and, on Claude Code, when it runs in the foreground without
.BR run_in_background :
either way nobody receives the watcher's result, while its watcher file still
satisfies
.BR agent-hook-stop (1).
A watcher chained in one call behind a push or pull-request creation (as in
.BR "git push && git pr-watch \-\-wait" )
gets advice to run the push first and the watcher alone in the background:
chained, the push is never recorded or re-armed.
.PP
The hook is agent-agnostic. Launchers identify the concrete runtime with
.B AGENTGUARD_NAME
and
.BR AGENTGUARD_SESSION_ID .
.PP
Execute this file directly so the kernel honors its privileged
.B /bin/bash -p
shebang. Direct executable invocation through that shebang is the
supported security boundary. Explicit interpreter invocation is outside this
security boundary:
Bash may process caller-controlled
.B BASH_ENV
code that changes or forges behavior before the script's first instruction.
No code in the script can undo or reliably detect those earlier effects.
Rejection of an otherwise ordinary, detectable explicit invocation is
best-effort only, not a security guarantee. Direct execution also requires a
trusted or sanitized dynamic-loader environment. On Linux, these controls
include
.BR LD_PRELOAD ,
.BR LD_AUDIT ,
and
.BR LD_LIBRARY_PATH .
These variables have platform equivalents, including macOS
.BR DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES ,
.BR DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH ,
and related
.B DYLD_*
controls. Those controls must likewise be removed or trusted before process
startup. The
.B -p
option changes Bash behavior only after the dynamic loader starts it;
.B -p
cannot protect before Bash loads. When Bash 4 or newer is required,
the launcher considers only
.BR /opt/homebrew/bin/bash ,
.BR /usr/local/bin/bash ,
.B /data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/bash
(Termux),
.BR /bin/bash ,
and
.BR /usr/bin/bash ,
in that order. Caller-controlled
.B HOME
and
.B PATH
cannot add interpreter candidates. These fixed interpreter paths, together
with the fixed platform Bash, awk, and env paths, form the launcher filesystem
trust boundary. Termux uses its corresponding fixed paths below
.BR /data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin ;
other platforms use
.BR /bin/bash ,
.BR /usr/bin/awk ,
and
.BR /usr/bin/env .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT
Seconds the classifier may spend on one command before the hook blocks it
(default 30, maximum 3600). Commands over 256 KiB, with more than 2048
commands, or with one command of more than 2048 words are also blocked,
because a hook that outlives the host timeout lets the command run unguarded.
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_WATCH
Set to
.B 0
to turn off the
.B git pr-watch
launch warnings.
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_WATCH_BLOCK
Set to
.B 1
to block those launches instead of warning.
.SH EXTENSIONS
After base policy runs, AgentGuard sources matching extension files through
its hook helper API. Extensions can add warnings, blocks, or context while the
base hook keeps ownership of the protocol response.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command is allowed, possibly with warnings or reminders.
.TP
.B 2
The command is blocked, or launcher initialization failed closed because its
interpreter, inspection tools, bootstrap protocol, or private descriptors were
unavailable or invalid. Any AgentGuard internal error, including an unexpected
exit status or a Bash runtime error raised inside AgentGuard's own code, is
also reported as a block.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentguard-classify-command (1),
.BR agent-hook-post-bash (1),
.BR wait-until (1)
